{"metadata": {"team_id": "Organizers", "run_id": "orga-ance-llama70b", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "1-1_7", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Acid reflux happens when the stomach acid travels back into your oesophagus. This can be due to a weak lower esophageal sphincter, which is the muscular region that acts as a boundary between the stomach and the esophagus tube. When the sphincter muscle relaxes at the wrong time, it allows stomach acid to back up into your esophagus, causing heartburn and other symptoms. It can also be caused by underlying health conditions such as gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD), ulcers, or gallbladder disease. Additionally, lifestyle factors such as eating too late, being overweight, and consuming trigger foods can also contribute to acid reflux. It's essential to consult with a medical professional to determine the underlying cause and develop a plan to manage symptoms.  Document 1 and 2 suggest that low stomach acid is not the underlying cause of acid reflux, but rather a weak lower esophageal sphincter or underlying health conditions. Document", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0017-40-03958:15": 7.664485454559326, "clueweb22-en0003-13-00040:9": 7.352406978607178, "clueweb22-en0008-79-15912:4": 6.630699157714844, "clueweb22-en0008-79-15912:0": 6.111818313598633, "clueweb22-en0003-37-07280:9": 5.990111351013184}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I eat dinner late at night.", "I have gained weight recently because I stopped exercising for the last 6 months.", "I want to change my lifestyle."]}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0017-40-03958:15": 7.664485454559326, "clueweb22-en0003-13-00040:9": 7.352406978607178, "clueweb22-en0008-79-15912:4": 6.630699157714844, "clueweb22-en0008-79-15912:0": 6.111818313598633, "clueweb22-en0003-37-07280:9": 5.990111351013184, "clueweb22-en0031-76-04262:5": 5.956017017364502, "clueweb22-en0018-89-02341:16": 5.902174949645996, "clueweb22-en0029-71-15430:0": 5.785346984863281, "clueweb22-en0028-31-14442:4": 5.7343