{"metadata": {"team_id": "Organizers", "run_id": "orga-ance-llama70b", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_9", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "To tape your finger, you can use a method that restricts the movement of the finger joint at the injured pulley(s) so that you can't crimp at all. This is called the H-taping method. To apply the H-taping, cut a length of rigid athletic tape 10cm long and 1.5cm wide. Place the tape on the injured finger with the bridge on the inside of the finger, sitting on the crease of the joint on the upper end of the injured pulley. Flex your finger 60\u00b0 at the joint being taped. Wrap the upper bit of the tape in a ring. Then wrap the other ring of tape below the joint. This will help support the injured pulley and prevent further injury. Remember to retape your injured digit between climbs or whenever you feel the tape slackening. This will ensure that the tape remains effective and provides the necessary support for your finger.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:13": 4.700679302215576, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:10": 4.566585540771484, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:2": 3.841989517211914, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:8": 3.7135508060455322, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:12": 3.414419651031494}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I am climbing regularly for the past 3 years.", "I climb outdoors."]}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:13": 4.700679302215576, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:10": 4.566585540771484, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:2": 3.841989517211914, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:8": 3.7135508060455322, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:12": 3.414419651031494, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:11": 2.601172924041748, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:6": 2.5992720127105713, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:9": 2.507782220840454, "clueweb22-en0006-32-19661:11": 2.4431774616241455, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:11": 2.283412456512451, "clueweb22-en0017-96-08323:5": 2.23665189743042, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:5": 2.1952943801879883, "clueweb22-en0033-87-0972